<%# Transient feedback for something that happened without a page load — a copy
    that succeeded, a bell that was cleared. `shared/_flashes` covers the other
    half: a message that has to survive a redirect. Neither is a fallback for
    the other.

    Any Stimulus controller raises one with

      this.dispatch("show", { prefix: "toast", detail: { message:, kind: } })

    and a controller action answering a Turbo request raises one with

      render turbo_stream: turbo_stream.toast(t("..."), kind: :success)

    The one action below routes every `toast:show` here, and the stream action
    in app/javascript/toast_stream.js raises exactly that event rather than
    writing a row of its own, so both callers share this template, this timer
    and this dismissal. A new caller needs no wiring in this file.

    The detail a caller may send:

      message   the sentence. Required; anything else raises nothing.
      kind      "info" (default), "success", "warning" or "error".
      title     an optional bold line above the message.
      duration  milliseconds before it leaves. 0 keeps it until dismissed.
      action    { label:, href:, method: } — one link out, e.g. an undo.

    The row is a <template> cloned per message rather than markup built in JS,
    the same shape as the proctor log's row. %>

<%# Where the stack sits. One of six, passed by the layout; the classes are
    written out per position rather than composed, because a class Tailwind
    cannot see as a literal is a class it does not build.

    `--toast-enter` is the offset a row animates in from, and it lives here
    rather than on the row so that a bottom-anchored stack rises into place
    instead of dropping into it. The controller reads it for both Web Animation
    keyframes, keeping position independent of variant ordering. %>
<% anchor, offset, place = {
     top_right:     [ "top", "-0.375rem", "top-(--toast-top) right-4 left-4 sm:left-auto" ],
     top_left:      [ "top", "-0.375rem", "top-(--toast-top) right-4 left-4 sm:right-auto" ],
     top_center:    [ "top", "-0.375rem", "top-(--toast-top) right-4 left-4 sm:right-auto sm:left-1/2 sm:-translate-x-1/2" ],
     bottom_right:  [ "bottom", "0.375rem", "right-4 bottom-4 left-4 sm:left-auto" ],
     bottom_left:   [ "bottom", "0.375rem", "right-4 bottom-4 left-4 sm:right-auto" ],
     bottom_center: [ "bottom", "0.375rem", "right-4 bottom-4 left-4 sm:right-auto sm:left-1/2 sm:-translate-x-1/2" ]
   }.fetch(local_assigns.fetch(:position, :top_right)) %>

<%# Below the sticky chrome, not over it: at top-4 a toast covered the header's
    language toggle and account menu for as long as it was up. `--toast-top`
    clears the marketing header — the same "header plus a gap" the base layer
    uses for scroll-padding-top — and the controller raises it to the measured
    height on a layout whose header is taller, which the app one is because the
    hearts strip sits inside it. A bottom-anchored stack has nothing to clear. %>
<div id="toasts"
     style="--toast-top: calc(var(--spacing-header) + var(--spacing) * 3); --toast-enter: <%= offset %>"
     data-controller="toast"
     data-toast-anchor-value="<%= anchor %>"
     data-action="toast:show@window->toast#show"
     class="pointer-events-none fixed z-50 sm:w-[380px] <%= place %>">
  <%# Two regions, because urgency is not a style. A screen reader interrupts
      for `assertive` and waits for `polite`, and "saved" should never interrupt
      anything. The controller files a row into the one its kind belongs to.

      aria-live on the region rather than the row: the row does not exist yet
      when the screen reader starts watching, and announcing needs a stable
      host. %>
  <div data-toast-target="list" data-urgency="polite" role="status" aria-live="polite"
       class="flex flex-col gap-2"></div>
  <div data-toast-target="list" data-urgency="assertive" role="alert" aria-live="assertive"
       class="flex flex-col gap-2 empty:hidden [&:not(:empty)]:mt-2"></div>

  <template data-toast-target="row">
    <%# pointer-events back on for the row only: the host spans the viewport so
        it can position the stack, and must not eat clicks meant for the page. %>
    <div data-kind="info" data-toast-row
         data-action="mouseenter->toast#hold mouseleave->toast#release
                      focusin->toast#hold focusout->toast#release"
         class="group pointer-events-auto flex items-start gap-2.5
                rounded-field border border-l-2 border-chrome-line bg-chrome px-3.5 py-2.75
                text-13-5 leading-[1.5] text-on-chrome-bright shadow-menu-dark
                data-[kind=success]:border-l-success data-[kind=warning]:border-l-gold
                data-[kind=error]:border-l-brand-alert">
      <%# One glyph per kind, shown by the variant rather than swapped in JS.
          Info has none: it is the default, and a mark for "nothing special
          happened" is noise. %>
      <span aria-hidden="true"
            class="hidden font-semibold text-success group-data-[kind=success]:inline">✓</span>
      <span aria-hidden="true"
            class="hidden font-semibold text-gold group-data-[kind=warning]:inline">!</span>
      <span aria-hidden="true"
            class="hidden font-semibold text-danger-bright group-data-[kind=error]:inline">✕</span>

      <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
        <p data-slot="title" hidden class="mb-0.5 font-semibold text-balance"></p>
        <p data-slot="message" class="text-balance"></p>

        <%# One way out of a toast and no more. The href and the method come from
            the caller; Turbo turns a non-GET into a real request. %>
        <a data-slot="action" hidden
           class="mt-1.5 inline-block font-medium text-danger-soft underline underline-offset-2
                  transition-colors hover:text-on-chrome-bright"></a>
      </div>

      <%# Always there, not only on a sticky toast: a message that is still being
          read should be dismissable by the person reading it. %>
      <button type="button" data-action="toast#close" data-slot="close"
              class="-mr-1 flex-none rounded px-1 leading-none text-on-chrome-muted
                     transition-colors hover:text-on-chrome-bright
                     focus-visible:ring-1 focus-visible:ring-chrome-edge focus-visible:outline-none">
        <span aria-hidden="true">✕</span>
        <span class="sr-only"><%= t("chrome.toast_dismiss") %></span>
      </button>
    </div>
  </template>
</div>
